<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bình Định province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bình Định is a coastal province of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> with its capital at Quy Nhơn. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> kingdom centred on Vijaya until the Vietnamese conquest of 1471, and in 1771 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> brothers began their rebellion from the village of Tây Sơn in its western uplands. It is also known for a local tradition of martial arts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/binh-dinh-province.jpg" alt="Bình Định province" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Minh28397 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Th%C3%A0nh_ph%E1%BB%91_Quy_Nh%C6%A1n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bình Định was a northern coastal province of the South Central Coast region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, bordering Quảng Ngãi to the north, Phú Yên to the south, Gia Lai to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> to the east. Most of it is covered by mountains or hills, rising to around 1,200 metres in An Lão district. The largest lowland lies in the south along the lower Côn River, which forms a small delta north of Quy Nhơn and can be used as an inland waterway. Most of the population and economic activity has long concentrated there, and it was the site of Vijaya, one of the major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bình Định was probably one of the places where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> first arrived in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. The favourable geography led to the rise of the Cham port of Thị Nại, where Quy Nhơn now stands, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of Vijaya grew up around this port and a main city further inland. Its architecture suggests it did not become important until the eleventh or twelfth century. It combined stone and brick, unlike most Cham structures, which used only bricks, which suggests some influence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>. Vijaya fought major wars with Angkor in the 12th and 13th centuries and with Vietnam in 1069 and again in the 15th century.</p><p>In 1471 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-thanh-tong">Lê Thánh Tông</a> won a decisive victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, ending Vijaya and the kingdom. He annexed its lands and set up the Quảng Nam protectorate with three prefectures, one of them Hoài Nhân, which corresponds to Bình Định. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn dynasty</a> took its name from Tây Sơn district, where its rulers came from, and Quy Nhơn was its capital until the Vietnamese civil war of 1789 to 1802 wracked the province and the dynasty collapsed.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>, Bình Định was the site of heavy fighting between 1966 and 1968, including Operation Masher, Operation Thayer and Operation Pershing. In February 1976 it was merged with Quảng Ngãi to form Nghĩa Bình, and it was separated again on 30 June 1989.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>On 12 June 2025 Bình Định province was dissolved and merged with Gia Lai province. It had 11 district-level subdivisions and its capital was Quy Nhơn. Cham towers are now significant tourist attractions, and the Quang Trung Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> district commemorates the Tây Sơn uprising. The traditional martial arts of Bình Định have become a cultural heritage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bình Định province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1771 the brothers Nguyen Nhac, Nguyen Hue and Nguyen Lu of Tay Son village in <b>Binh Dinh</b> rose against the Nguyen lords, attracting peasants, merchants and highland peoples with promises to redistribute wealth.</blockquote><a href="/state/tay-son-dynasty">Tay Son dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
